prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a military formation simulator. Load 12 soldier models and support switching among multiple formations and marching simulation.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import through ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/RobotExpressive.glb;
(2) RobotExpressive.glb contains skeletal animations (idle, walk/run, etc.); iterate over gltf.animations to obtain the animation clips;
(3) Use SkeletonUtils.clone (requires import { clone } from 'three/addons/utils/SkeletonUtils.js') to clone 12 independent soldier instances;
(4) The initial arrangement is a rectangular formation of 3 rows × 4 columns, with 2-meter spacing, centered in the scene;
(5) Each soldier has an independent AnimationMixer;
(6) After loading is complete, set window.__3D_STATE__.modelLoaded = true;

[Scene]
(7) A sufficiently large green ground plane and a sky-blue background of 0x87CEEB;
(8) DirectionalLight + AmbientLight illumination;
(9) An OrbitControls camera overlooking the formation from above at a 45-degree angle, with the initial position (0, 15, 15);

[Formation System]
(10) 4 formation buttons:
  - "Line" button (id="lineBtn"): arrange all 12 soldiers in one horizontal line with uniform spacing;
  - "Square Formation" button (id="squareBtn"): a rectangular formation of 3 rows × 4 columns;
  - "Arrow" button (id="arrowBtn"): a V-shaped formation, with the point facing forward and both wings spreading backward;
  - "Circle Formation" button (id="circleBtn"): distribute all 12 soldiers evenly around a circle, facing the center;
(11) When switching formations, soldiers must transition smoothly from their current positions to their target positions using lerp interpolation, completing the transition in approximately 2 seconds;
(12) Play the walk animation during the transition, then switch back to the idle animation after reaching the target positions;
(13) Soldiers face the direction of movement and, after arrival, face the front of the formation; in the circle formation, they face the center of the circle;

[Marching Mode]
(14) "March" toggle button (id="marchBtn"): click to enable or disable marching mode;
(15) In marching mode, all soldiers maintain the current formation and move forward together in the positive z direction at approximately 1 m/s;
(16) All soldiers play the walk animation while marching and switch back to idle when marching stops;
(17) The marching direction remains unchanged, and the formation shape remains stable while moving;

[Spacing Control]
(18) Spacing slider (id="spacingSlider", range input, min=1, max=3, step=0.1, default 2);
(19) When the spacing is adjusted, update soldier positions smoothly by recalculating the target positions for the current formation;

[HUD Display]
(20) The HUD in the upper-left corner displays:
  - Current formation name (id="formationName")
  - Marching state (id="marchState", displaying "Marching" or "Stationary")
  - Current spacing (id="spacingValue")
  - Soldier count (id="soldierCount", which must be 12)

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
{
  modelLoaded: boolean,
  formation: string,
  marching: boolean,
  spacing: number,
  soldierCount: number,
  soldierPositions: Array<{x: number, z: number}>,
  transitioning: boolean,
  marchDistance: number
}

Output a single HTML file, place it in the same directory as the assets/ folder, and run it through a local HTTP server.
